# The tree says what happened; the diff says what it left behind. A consuming
# exercise is the archive -- a transaction tree carries no archived event -- so
# the archived side is derived, which is the part worth pinning.

# RUN: printf 'diff\nq\n' | %dpm open %root/examples/exercise-child-create.trace.json --visualize --color never | FileCheck %s --check-prefix SPLIT
# RUN: printf 'diff\nq\n' | %dpm open %root/examples/create.trace.json --visualize --color never | FileCheck %s --check-prefix CREATE
# RUN: printf 'diff\nq\n' | %dpm open %root/tests/fixtures/reassignment/real-unassign-artifact.json --visualize --color never | FileCheck %s --check-prefix EMPTY

# Split archives the original and creates two; the payloads tell them apart.
# SPLIT: state diff: 2 contracts created, 1 contract archived
# SPLIT: + created
# SPLIT: quantity: 60
# SPLIT: quantity: 40
# SPLIT: x archived
# SPLIT: visible to Issuer only; the transaction may have touched other contracts

# CREATE: state diff: 1 contract created, 0 contracts archived
# CREATE-NOT: x archived

# An update touching no contract here says so, rather than printing an empty
# panel that looks broken.
# EMPTY: no contract was created or archived in this projection
